WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.884
Welcome to Horizon installer training.

2
00:00:03.884 --> 00:00:07.172
This is Part 1: Class
2, and what it means.

3
00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:10.903
By the end, you'll know why
amps matter more than volts,

4
00:00:10.903 --> 00:00:15.134
how to work out amps yourself,
and the words we use on every job.

5
00:00:16.284 --> 00:00:18.432
Here's the whole standard in one line.

6
00:00:18.732 --> 00:00:22.449
Every light circuit we install
carries 4 amps or less.

7
00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:26.366
This part shows you why. The
rest of the course shows you how.

8
00:00:27.516 --> 00:00:30.520
Start with two words: volts and amps.

9
00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:33.020
Volts are how hard the power pushes.

10
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:36.813
Amps are how much power is
actually moving through the wire.

11
00:00:37.113 --> 00:00:41.524
It's the amps that make heat. Push
too many amps down a thin wire,

12
00:00:41.524 --> 00:00:44.581
and it gets hot. Hot enough, and it burns.

13
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.950
The wire built into our light
strings is thin. It's 20 gauge,

14
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:53.536
and it can only take a few amps.
Protecting that wire is the job.

15
00:00:54.686 --> 00:00:57.636
So how do you work out
amps? You need two numbers,

16
00:00:57.636 --> 00:01:02.192
and both are printed on the power
supply label. The watts, and the volts.

17
00:01:02.492 --> 00:01:06.479
Then divide. Amps equals
watts, divided by volts.

18
00:01:06.779 --> 00:01:14.104
Try it on our 600 watt supply. 600
watts, divided by 24 volts, is 25 amps.

19
00:01:14.404 --> 00:01:18.855
Our 450 watt supply works the same way.

20
00:01:18.855 --> 00:01:24.354
450 divided by 24 is
18.75 amps. Call it 18.8.

21
00:01:24.654 --> 00:01:28.600
That's how many amps the supply
can push out if nothing stops it.

22
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.431
Either way, it's far more than
thin 20 gauge wire can take.

23
00:01:32.731 --> 00:01:37.172
Now try one circuit.
Our limit is 96 watts,

24
00:01:37.172 --> 00:01:41.552
at 24 volts. 96 divided by 24 is 4 amps.

25
00:01:41.852 --> 00:01:46.312
It works on any supply you ever
look at, ours or anyone else's.

26
00:01:46.312 --> 00:01:51.800
Read the watts and the volts off the
label, divide, and you know the amps.

27
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.065
So what keeps 25 amps away
from a thin wire? A breaker.

28
00:01:57.365 --> 00:02:02.588
A breaker is a switch that shuts the power
off by itself when it sees too many amps.

29
00:02:02.888 --> 00:02:06.105
Every light circuit gets
its own 4 amp breaker.

30
00:02:06.105 --> 00:02:11.764
The supply can still push 25 amps, but
no single circuit ever gets more than 4.

31
00:02:12.064 --> 00:02:14.616
That drop, from what the supply can push,

32
00:02:14.616 --> 00:02:17.668
down to 4 amps a circuit,
is the whole point.

33
00:02:18.818 --> 00:02:22.194
So what does Class 2 actually
mean? It's a safety rule.

34
00:02:22.494 --> 00:02:27.671
A Class 2 circuit can only carry a
small amount of power. About 100 watts.

35
00:02:27.971 --> 00:02:31.549
At 24 volts, 100 watts is about 4.16 amps.

36
00:02:31.549 --> 00:02:36.400
Our 4 amp breaker keeps every
circuit just under that.

37
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.204
Keep the power that small,

38
00:02:38.204 --> 00:02:43.196
and a short or a damaged wire doesn't have
enough power behind it to start a fire.

39
00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:46.041
Our lights are built
for Class 2 power only.

40
00:02:46.041 --> 00:02:50.399
Feed them from a bigger circuit, and
their safety rating means nothing.

41
00:02:50.699 --> 00:02:55.299
The lights are only as safe as the
circuit feeding them. That part is on us.

42
00:02:56.449 --> 00:02:59.321
Last, the words you'll hear on every job.

43
00:02:59.621 --> 00:03:02.784
Watt. Total power. Volts times amps.

44
00:03:03.084 --> 00:03:07.371
Circuit. One breaker, one
wire, and one group of lights.

45
00:03:07.671 --> 00:03:10.767
Feeder. The wire from the
box out to the lights.

46
00:03:11.067 --> 00:03:15.081
Injection. A spot where we
feed fresh power into the run.

47
00:03:15.381 --> 00:03:17.952
And PW. That's Pure White in the app.

48
00:03:17.952 --> 00:03:22.372
All three colours are on at
once, so it uses the most power.

49
00:03:23.522 --> 00:03:24.546
Let's lock it in.

50
00:03:24.846 --> 00:03:26.898
Amps make heat, not volts.

51
00:03:27.198 --> 00:03:31.192
Amps equals watts divided by
volts. Read both off the label.

52
00:03:31.492 --> 00:03:34.557
Every light circuit gets
its own 4 amp breaker.

53
00:03:34.857 --> 00:03:37.877
Class 2 means a small
circuit, about 100 watts.

54
00:03:37.877 --> 00:03:40.569
That is what our lights are built for.

55
00:03:40.869 --> 00:03:45.393
Next, take the Part 1 quiz.
Then Part 2 is planning the job.
